{
    "title": "III National Conference on Aviation Psychology",
    "description": "<p>The III National Conference on Aviation Psychology is an event dedicated to dialogue between academia, civil institutions, and military organisations on the topics of aviation and aerospace psychology. The third edition follows the path traced by the previous editions held in Rome and Florence, building on the accumulated experience while opening up to new perspectives.</p> <p>The rapid evolution of the aeronautical sector &mdash; marked by increasing automation of systems, the integration of advanced digital technologies, and emerging challenges related to the human factor &mdash; requires ever more dynamic and interdisciplinary approaches. Milan, a privileged hub of technological innovation and scientific research, confirms itself as a natural venue for a conference that aims to bring together diverse expertise and promote an up-to-date reflection on the state of the discipline.</p> <p>The proceedings are structured around <em>three</em> thematic panels, each dedicated to a specific dimension of aviation psychology, in a journey that moves from theoretical and regulatory foundations to operational implications in professional practice.</p> <p>The <strong><em>Knowledge panel</em></strong> explores the epistemological, historical, and regulatory foundations of aviation psychology. The scientific roots of the discipline and its evolution in parallel with the aeronautical sector are reviewed, with particular attention to the national and international regulatory frameworks that define its professional boundaries. Ample space is given to the topic of accreditation and training of aviation psychologists &mdash; an area still being defined at the national level &mdash; on which the conference aims to foster a constructive dialogue among institutions, practitioners, and academia.</p> <p>The <strong><em>Research panel</em></strong> is dedicated to the presentation and discussion of the most recent scientific contributions in the field of aviation psychology. Research spans multiple areas of inquiry: from pilot psychophysiology under stress conditions to cognitive dynamics in highly automated cockpits, from crew resilience assessment to emergency management, through to applications of cognitive neuroscience to flight safety. The panel also provides an opportunity for methodological comparison across the plurality of approaches &mdash; quantitative, qualitative, experimental, clinical, and organisational &mdash; that characterise scientific output in the sector.</p> <p>The <strong><em>Applications panel</em></strong> is dedicated to operational practices and tools through which aviation psychology translates into the daily work of psychologists, physicians, instructors, and safety managers. Models for the psychological selection and assessment of aeronautical personnel are discussed, with attention to new emerging professional profiles and methodologies for evaluating transversal competencies such as workload management, decision-making, and team communication. Space is also given to psychological support programmes, mental health prevention, and wellbeing promotion in aeronautical organisations, in light of the most recent international regulations on mental health in high-risk work environments.</p>",
    "objectives": [
        "<strong>Consolidate the identity of the discipline</strong>, by exploring the epistemological, historical, and regulatory principles that define its scientific foundations",
        "<strong>Review the national and international regulatory framework</strong> governing the profession, highlighting established areas and those still to be developed",
        "<strong>Contribute to the definition of an accreditation/training system</strong> for aviation psychologists, still being developed at the national level",
        "<strong>Present and discuss the most recent research in the field</strong>, promoting methodological comparison across different approaches",
        "<strong>Showcase operational practices</strong>, comparing models and tools used in the selection, assessment, and support of aeronautical personnel",
        "<strong>Foster interdisciplinary dialogue</strong> between academia, civil institutions, and military organisations on topics in aeronautical psychology"
    ],
